{"name":"Gazelle","hex":"#947e68","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Gazelle","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A census-designated place in Siskiyou County, California, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Gazelle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gazelle","blocks":[{"text":"A gazelle is one of many antelope species in the genus Gazella. There are also seven species included in two further genera; Eudorcas and Nanger, which were formerly considered subgenera of Gazella. A third former subgenus, Procapra, includes three living species of Asian gazelles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Chinkara_-_Shreeram_M_V_-_Bikaner.jpg/330px-Chinkara_-_Shreeram_M_V_-_Bikaner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazelle","wikidata":"Q29001815"},{"title":"Gazelle (1854 sidewheeler)","blocks":[{"text":"Gazelle was an early sidewheeler on the Willamette River in what is now the U.S. state of Oregon. She did not operate long, suffering a catastrophic boiler explosion on April 8, 1854, less than a month after her trial voyage. This was the worst such explosion ever to occur in the Pacific Northwest states. The wrecked Gazelle was rebuilt and operated for a few years, first briefly as the unpowered barge Sarah Hoyt and then, with boilers installed, as the steamer Señorita. A victim of the explosion was D.P. Fuller, age 28, who is buried in Lone Fir Cemetery in Portland, Oregon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Gazelle_%28steamboat%29_explosion.jpg/330px-Gazelle_%28steamboat%29_explosion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazelle_(1854_sidewheeler)","wikidata":"Q5529063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Mosaic_of_a_gazelle_Ceasarea_israel.jpg/500px-Mosaic_of_a_gazell